{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And he knew what his youngest son had done to him.</b> Ham was the youngest of his sons, and how is it that he says, 'Cursed be Canaan'? He should have cursed Ham, who was the perpetrator of the deed. And the reason he did not curse him is for two reasons. The first is that it was not possible to curse Ham, because the Holy One, blessed be He, had already blessed him, as it is said, ״ויברך אלהים את נח ואת בניו״. The second is that if he had cursed Ham, it would have harmed only his own person but not his seed; therefore he wished to curse Canaan, who was Ham's firstborn son, so that if he afterward begat a thousand sons, all his seed would be included in the curse. And as for what Scripture says, ״ובני חם כוש ומצרים ופוט וכנען״ — which makes it appear that Canaan was the youngest of them all — this was after the curse: he gave precedence to all his brothers over him; therefore he mentioned him last. And the proof that Canaan was Ham's firstborn is His saying ״וירא חם אבי כנען״ — and He had already said above ״וחם הוא אבי כנען״. But surely at the time of this deed Ham had no offspring other than Canaan alone, and so he is his firstborn."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:29.670269964Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}